+### Cancelled Step 2: singleton admission and historical reads — October 17, 2026
+
+Step 2 grouped slide feedback is permanently cancelled. New slide feedback uses
+independent singleton `POST /api/attempts/{id}/slide-review/` requests and Step 1
+`selected-review-context-v1`. Multi-slide mutations are rejected at the shared
+service boundary, regardless of environment variables or protocol headers. The
+experimental `/slide-review/initial/` endpoint and grouping feature flag are removed.
+There is no grouping dependency in whole-presentation `score_` admission.
+
+Each automatic initial request retains its per-slide durable claim, current-source
+and correction checks, shared submission lock, and uncertain-outcome recovery.
+Navigation, reopening, saved reads and correction staleness do not authorize retries
+or score generation. Existing independent reviews keep their revision, selection,
+transcript and explicit retry/uncertainty controls.
+
+Historical grouped feedback remains readable through a singleton GET projection.
+Exact saved singleton analyses take precedence over a legacy group. A projection
+may retain optional `group_owner` metadata identifying its original owner; this
+never authorizes group expansion or coupled updates. An explicit independent
+Update from that projection omits the old group's `feedback_revision`, retains
+current selection/transcript assertions, and creates a distinct singleton owner.
+Historical projections add nullable `independent_selection` and
+`independent_availability` for that new singleton operation. Saved `selection` and
+`availability` continue to describe the original owner; they are not overwritten
+when provider configuration changes. Missing/invalid independent metadata fails
+closed until a fresh compatible GET.
+Active or unresolved historical work blocks this handoff with
+`review_history_pending`; it is not silently retried. Once the singleton exists,
+late historical projections cannot overwrite it in the client cache.
+
+Migration `0010_slide_review_membership` and its model remain as historical schema;
